    "title": "When Nature Strikes: Learning from Natural Disasters",
    "passage": "Natural disasters have shaped human history in powerful ways. From the shaking of the earth to the roaring of the sea, nature can damage homes and lives within minutes. Yet understanding these events helps us prepare for them.\n\nEarthquakes are among the most feared disasters. Scientists measure their strength by magnitude, a number that tells us how much energy is released. A high-magnitude quake can cause a landslide on a mountainside, sending rocks and soil rushing down. It can also make the ground slide beneath buildings, turning solid streets into dangerous slopes. In coastal areas, an undersea earthquake may produce a tsunami, a series of huge waves that can flood towns far inland. When a tsunami reaches landfall, the water may rise several metres in minutes, sweeping away cars and boats.\n\nStorms bring their own threats. A tornado is a violently spinning column of air that can destroy everything in its path. Heavy rain from a storm can cause a flood, while a long period without rain leads to a drought that dries up rivers and ruins crops. In some regions, a volcanic eruption covers the land with ash and hot lava, forcing thousands of people to leave their homes.\n\nAfter any disaster, the first task is rescue. Teams search for survivors, and volunteers bring food and water. Governments then work to repair the damage and help communities rebuild. Schools teach children what to do during an emergency, and early warning systems give people time to reach safety.\n\nNo one can stop a tornado or a tsunami, but knowledge saves lives. By studying how these events happen, we can reduce their harm and build a safer future.",
